issue.body:
## What's happening

In `fixtures/example_design/cpu_top.sv`, two connections silently drop out of the diagram even though the source is completely ordinary SystemVerilog:

```verilog
// bit-selects used directly in the port list
control_unit u_control_unit (
  .opcode (instr[7:4]),   // no edge into u_control_unit
  ...
);
register_file u_register_file (
  .rs1_addr (instr[3:2]), // no edge into u_register_file
  .rs2_addr (instr[1:0]), // no edge into u_register_file
  .rd_addr  (instr[3:2]), // no edge into u_register_file
  ...
);

// a literal constant used directly in the port list
adder #(.WIDTH(8)) u_pc_adder (
  .a   (pc),
  .b   (8'd1),            // renders with no wire at all
  .sum (pc_plus_1)
);
```

`instr` only shows a wire to `u_imm_gen` (whole-signal connection); the bit-sliced fan-out into `u_control_unit`/`u_register_file` has no wire, no bus-breakout node — nothing. `u_pc_adder.b` shows the `8'd1` text but no literal node/wire, i.e. looks unconnected.

## Root cause

Both are the same backend gap, not two separate bugs. `DesignExtractor::buildEdges` (`src/parser/backend_cpp/src/extractor_parts/lookup_source.inc:44-154`) groups ports into edges by exact string match on `signal`. When an instance port's `.name(expr)` is a bit-select or a literal, `expr` (e.g. `"instr[7:4]"`, `"8'd1"`) becomes the port's `connectedSignal` (set in `modules.inc` around line 233, `np.signal = getSignalName(high)`), but nothing else in the design produces an output port with that exact literal string as its signal — so the signal has loads but no driver, and no edge is emitted.

For `assign`-based bit-selects/literals (e.g. `assign x = y[3:0];`), `processAssign` explicitly synthesizes a bus-breakout node or literal node (`ensureLiteralNode`, the array/bus breakout helpers) that becomes the driver. That synthesis step only runs for continuous/procedural assignments — the instance-port-connection loop in `modules.inc` (~line 195-290) builds `NodePort.signal` directly from the connection expression and never calls the equivalent breakout/literal synthesis, so any design that slices or hardcodes a value **directly in an instantiation's port list** (very common, idiomatic SV) loses that connection from the diagram entirely.

### Minimal repro (unrelated to the CPU design)

```verilog
module sub (input logic [3:0] a, input logic [3:0] b, output logic [3:0] y);
  assign y = a + b;
endmodule

module top (input logic [7:0] data, output logic [3:0] result);
  sub u_sub (
    .a (data[7:4]),  // bit-select inline at instantiation
    .b (4'd1),       // literal inline at instantiation
    .y (result)
  );
endmodule
```

Built via `buildDesignGraph`/`buildViewModel`, the resulting view has exactly one edge (`u_sub -> result`); `a` and `b` have no edges and no synthesized bus-breakout/literal nodes.

## Suggested fix direction

In the instance-port-connection loop (`modules.inc`), when `high` (the high-conn expression) is a bit-select/part-select, run it through the same breakout-node synthesis `processAssign` uses; when it's a literal, call `ensureLiteralNode` for it — the same way both are already handled for `assign` RHS values. Then `buildEdges` will have a driver to match against and emit the edge normally.

## Where this was found

Investigated while reviewing #126 (adds `fixtures/example_design/`). Not a defect in that PR's SystemVerilog — `cpu_top.sv` just happens to exercise a pattern (bit-selects/literals inline at instantiation) that isn't covered by any existing fixture/test.
